<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Epidii</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of Kintyre in western Scotland recorded by Ptolemy, whose name means 'horse people' and whose land later became the heart of Dál Riata.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Ptolemy's Geography</a> of about 150 places the Epidii in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a> peninsula and neighbouring islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mull-of-kintyre">Mull of Kintyre</a> named Epidion Akron after them. Their name comes from the Brittonic word for horse, suggesting a people who honoured horses or a horse deity.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is known of their rulers or institutions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a> and the islands were a land of duns, crannogs and small forts, with the sea as the main highway. The peninsula lies only about 20 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">Antrim</a>, and contact with Ireland was constant across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-channel">North Channel</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Epidii disappear from the record after Ptolemy. By the 6th century their region was ruled by the Gaelic kings of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, whose territory spanned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> and north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">Antrim</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a> was held by its leading kindred, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cenel-ngabrain">Cenél nGabráin</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/epidii.jpg" alt="Epidii" width="320" height="464"><figcaption>Image: (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.north.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=epidii" aria-label="Epidii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4611 315 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Epidii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 571h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4931 571h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=epidii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

